The '''McLaren Speedtail''' is a limited production [[Hybrid vehicle|hybrid]] [[sports car]] manufactured by [[McLaren Automotive]], revealed on October 26, 2018. This car is the fourth edition in the ''McLaren Ultimate Series'', after the [[McLaren Senna|Senna]], the [[McLaren P1|P1]], and the [[McLaren F1|F1]]. The car is also part of the 18 new cars or derivatives that McLaren will launch as part of its ''Track22'' business plan.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2016-07-22 |title=McLaren on course for 15 new cars by 2022 |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/mclaren-course-15-new-cars-2022 |access-date=2022-06-09 |website=Top Gear |language=en}}</ref>

== Specifications ==
The Speedtail is powered by a version of the [[McLaren M840T engine|M840T]] twin turbocharged V8 engine from the [[McLaren 720S|720S]], upgraded to produce {{convert|756|PS|hp kW|0|abbr=on|order=flip}} and {{convert|590|lbft|Nm|0|abbr=on}} of torque. The engine is combined with a hybrid powertrain producing an extra {{convert|312|PS|kW bhp|0|abbr=on}} to generate a total power output of {{convert|1036|hp|kW PS|0|abbr=on}} and {{convert|848|lbft|Nm|0|abbr=on}} of torque.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.motorauthority.com/news/1121842_mclaren-speedtail-specs-make-it-a-worthy-f1-successor|title=McLaren Speedtail specs make it a worthy F1 successor|date=4 March 2018|publisher=Motor Authority|access-date=27 October 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://paultan.org/2018/10/26/mclaren-speedtail-revealed-in-full-before-official-debut/|title=McLaren Speedtail revealed in full before official debut|date=26 October 2018|publisher=|access-date=27 October 2018}}</ref> The Speedtail uses a [[Carbon fibers|carbon fibre]] [[monocoque]], with the passenger seats integrated into the [[chassis]], as well as dihedral doors like other McLaren models. McLaren claims that the Speedtail has a top speed of {{convert|403|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}, can accelerate from {{Convert|0-60|mph|km/h|0|abbr=on|order=}} in 2.9 seconds, {{Convert|0-100|mph|km/h|0|abbr=on|order=}} in 5.1 seconds, and {{Convert|0-186|mph|km/h|0|abbr=on|order=flip}} in 12.8 seconds.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=McLaren Speedtail - Hybrid Hyper-GT & the Fastest McLaren |url=https://cars.mclaren.com/ultimate-series/mclaren-speedtail}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |title=McLaren Speedtail Unveiled: Heir To The Throne |url=https://www.motor1.com/news/273475/mclaren-speedtail-hypercar-unveiled/ |access-date=27 October 2018 |publisher=}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2018-10-26 |title=This is the 250mph McLaren Speedtail |url=https://www.topgear.com/car-news/supercars/250mph-mclaren-speedtail |access-date=2018-11-02 |work=Top Gear |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=MacKenzie |first=Angus |title=2021 McLaren Speedtail: The Only Car in the World That Can Take You and Two Friends to 250 mph |url=https://www.motortrend.com/reviews/2021-mclaren-speedtail-first-drive/}}</ref>

== Technology ==
The Speedtail recharges its hybrid battery while driving, though a wireless charging pad is included with the car, trickle-charging it when not in use.<ref name="Speed"/>

McLaren says that one of the main goals of the Speedtail was to minimize drag.<ref name=":0" /> To achieve this, the design is inspired by the shape of a teardrop and features carbon fibre static covers on the front wheels. It also features hydraulically actuated active rear [[Flight control surfaces|aerodynamic control surfaces]], which are formed in flexible carbon fibre and are an integral part of the rear clamshell.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ballaban |first=Michael |title=The 2019 McLaren Speedtail Has Flexible Carbon Fiber That Bends and It Goes 250 MPH |url=https://jalopnik.com/the-2019-mclaren-speedtail-has-flexible-carbon-fiber-th-1830020270 |access-date=2018-11-02 |work=Jalopnik |language=en-US}}</ref> McLaren says this was chosen over a traditional rear spoiler in order to reduce drag. The Speedtail is fitted with [[Smart glass|electrochromic glass]], which darkens at the push of a button, eliminating the need for [[sun visor]]s. Similarly, the Speedtail does not feature door mirrors, instead using HD cameras mounted on the front guards that pop out when the ignition is turned on and retract inside when the "Velocity mode" is activated, which reduces overall drag and optimizes performance.<ref name="Engadget">{{Cite news |title=McLaren's $2.25 million Speedtail hybrid boasts 250MPH speeds |url=https://www.engadget.com/2018/10/26/mclaren-speedtail-hybrid-250mph-speeds/ |access-date=2018-11-02 |work=Engadget |language=en-US}}</ref>

== Interior ==
The Speedtail has a 3-seat layout, similar to the preceding [[McLaren F1|F1]], which has the driver sitting at the centre of the car, and slightly forward of the two passenger seats. On the original F1, this layout was used to provide better visibility than a conventional seating layout. The interior of the Speedtail features "directional leather finish" which McLaren says, “makes it easy to slide into the seat but then subtly holds the occupant in place while they drive.” and is strong enough that it can be used in place of carpet on the floor of the Speedtail. It also features "Titanium Deposition Carbon Fibre", which is when "a micron-thin layer of titanium is fused directly onto the weave and becomes an integral part of the carbon fibre’s construction.", as well as Thin-Ply Technology Carbon Fibre (TPT), consisting of countless 30 micron thick layers of carbon fibre.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://media.mclarenautomotive.com/releases/625#gallery|title=McLaren Newsroom|website=media.mclarenautomotive.com|access-date=2018-11-02|archive-date=12 April 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190412200111/http://media.mclarenautomotive.com/releases/625#gallery|url-status=dead}}</ref> The company also offers bespoke luggage for Speedtail owners, a practice implemented when the F1 went on sale.<ref name="Engadget"/>

== Production ==
Testing was done in [[Florida]] at the [[Kennedy Space Center]] at the Johnny Bohmer Proving Grounds. The shuttle landing runway was used for the tests. It was also tested on tracks in [[Germany]], [[Spain]] and [[Italy]].<ref name="Speed">{{cite web |last1=Cole |first1=Craig |title=McLaren Speedtail prototype hits 250-mph top speed more than 30 times|url=https://www.cnet.com/roadshow/news/mclaren-speedtail-acceleration-performance/|website=Cnet |access-date=24 December 2019}}</ref>

In November 2018, McLaren planned to build 106 examples of the Speedtail at an MSRP of around £2.1 million. Due in part to its use of cameras in place of side mirrors and no side-mounted airbags, the Speedtail does not meet [[Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standards]] (FMVSS) in the [[United States]], even though around 35% of the total cars were bought by American buyers. A spokeswoman for the company stated that the car may be legal, pending approval by the [[NHTSA]], to be imported into the U.S. under the “[[Show or Display]]” law, which exempts cars that are “historically or technologically significant" from FMVSS, but imposes a mileage limit of 2,500 miles in a 12-month period and registration of the vehicle with the [[United States Department of Transportation|DOT]]. McLaren has made it clear that they will not offer assistance with importing or registering the Speedtail in the U.S.<ref name="legality">{{Cite news|url=https://jalopnik.com/the-2019-mclaren-speedtail-isnt-quite-legal-in-the-u-s-1830027062|title=The 2019 McLaren Speedtail Isn't Quite Legal in the U.S.|last=Lee|first=Kristen|work=Jalopnik|access-date=2018-11-02|language=en-US}}</ref>

Production of the McLaren Speedtail commenced in the [[United Kingdom]] after high-speed testing was completed by December 2019. The prototype XP2 version had "reached its terminal velocity more than 30 times," topping out at {{convert|403|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}}, and able to go from a standstill to {{convert|300|km/h|mph|0|abbr=on|order=flip}} in less than 13 seconds. The first deliveries, 106 cars, were slated for February 2020 to [[Woking, England]].<ref name="Speed"/>

